<div class="journal-header">
  <div class="journal-name">International Journal of Web Engineering &amp; Business Information Systems</div>
  <div>Vol. 12 | Issue 2 | June 2026 | ISSN: 2456-XXXX</div>
</div>

<div class="article-title">ProTech: A Containerised Django Marketplace for Professional Services with Escrow Payments, Role-Based Access Control, and Two-Sided Monetisation</div>
<div class="article-subtitle">An End-of-Year Master's Project in IT Innovation in Business</div>
<div class="authors"><strong>Manye Patrice</strong></div>
<div class="affiliation">
  IT Innovation in Business — Master&#x27;s Degree<br>
  Web Technology in Business · Web Application Development<br>
  Email: manye.patrice@university.edu | Repository: github.com/logandescapo-eng/protech1
</div>

<hr class="divider">

<div class="abstract-box">
  <div class="abstract-title">ABSTRACT</div>
  <p>This paper presents ProTech, a full-stack professional services marketplace developed as an end-of-year
  master's project within the IT Innovation in Business programme. The platform connects clients with skilled
  service workers—plumbers, electricians, cleaners, and related trades—through a unified web application that
  supports discovery, booking, in-app messaging, post-job reviews, and escrow-protected payments. Unlike an
  earlier prototype built with PHP and MySQL, the current system implements a production-oriented architecture
  using Django 5.2.15, PostgreSQL 15, Redis 7, Gunicorn, and Nginx orchestrated via Docker Compose. Security
  is enforced through PBKDF2 password hashing, CSRF middleware, ORM parameterisation, template auto-escaping,
  and a custom role-based access decorator separating client, worker, and administrator flows. A dedicated
  escrow subsystem in <code>escrow/services.py</code> manages wallet balances, held funds, platform commission
  (default five percent), and auditable transaction records using Decimal arithmetic and atomic database
  transactions. The paper describes the three-tier deployment model, relational schema spanning four Django
  applications, hybrid business model combining transaction commissions and worker subscription tiers,
  Redis-backed caching strategy, structured logging, and a seventeen-test automated suite executed in GitHub
  Actions CI. Evaluation demonstrates that the platform meets academic full-stack development requirements
  while remaining clone-and-run for live demonstration. Limitations—including demo-only payments and
  subscription logic not yet coded—are stated explicitly, and future work toward Stripe integration and
  real-time notifications is outlined.</p>
  <div class="keywords">
    <strong>Keywords:</strong> Django, marketplace platform, escrow payments, role-based access control,
    Docker Compose, PostgreSQL, Redis caching, two-sided markets, full-stack web development, service economy
  </div>
</div>

<hr class="divider">

<div class="two-col-layout">

<h2>1. Introduction</h2>
<p>The digital transformation of professional services has created substantial demand for platforms that connect clients with verified workers while reducing search friction, payment risk, and administrative overhead. Established marketplaces such as TaskRabbit, Thumbtack, and Upwork demonstrate that two-sided platforms can aggregate supply and demand at scale; however, local tradespeople and small businesses often lack affordable, transparent channels to build reputation and receive secure online payments.</p>
<p>ProTech addresses this gap through an academically rigorous yet commercially informed implementation. Developed by Manye Patrice as the capstone for Web Technology in Business and Web Application Development, the platform serves clients who browse, book, and pay for services, and workers who manage profiles, accept jobs, and receive wallet-based payouts. The project evolved from an initial PHP/MySQL prototype—documented in prior coursework—into a Django-centred system aligned with contemporary enterprise web practice and the module assessment rubric.</p>
<p>This article contributes four elements to the web engineering literature in an academic context. First, it documents a complete migration from a monolithic PHP prototype to a domain-partitioned Django application, including a Jinja2 compatibility layer preserving template semantics. Second, it presents an internal escrow ledger design suitable for marketplace trust without immediate third-party payment integration. Third, it maps functional requirements to verifiable implementation evidence through an explicit rubric alignment framework. Fourth, it combines technical architecture with a hybrid monetisation model grounded in two-sided market theory.</p>

<h2>2. Related Work</h2>
<p>Research on electronic commerce adoption emphasises perceived usefulness, ease of use, and trust as primary drivers of platform participation (Pavlou &amp; Fygenson, 2006). In service marketplaces, trust mechanisms—including verified identity, reputation systems, and payment holding—reduce perceived transaction risk (Edelman et al., 2012). ProTech operationalises these insights through role-separated dashboards, booking-linked reviews, and escrow holds released only upon job completion.</p>
<p>Platform economics literature formalises pricing on both sides of a market (Rochet &amp; Tirole, 2003; Evans &amp; Schmalensee, 2016). ProTech&#x27;s business design—free client access, worker commissions, and optional subscription tiers—follows the asymmetric pricing pattern common among services marketplaces where supply-side participants derive ongoing value from leads, scheduling tools, and payment infrastructure.</p>
<p>From a software engineering perspective, Fielding (2000) and Fowler (2002) inform the separation of presentation, domain logic, and persistence. Django&#x27;s integrated ORM, migration system, admin interface, and security middleware embody enterprise patterns appropriate for a assessed full-stack project. Containerisation via Docker Compose reflects reproducible deployment practice described in contemporary DevOps documentation (Docker Inc., 2024). Security design references the OWASP Top Ten (2021), addressing broken access control, injection, and cryptographic failures through RBAC decorators, ORM queries, and PBKDF2 hashing.</p>

<h2>3. System Architecture</h2>
<p>ProTech adopts a three-tier architecture deployed as four Docker Compose services: PostgreSQL (db), Redis (redis), Django/Gunicorn (backend), and Nginx (nginx). The presentation tier delivers server-rendered HTML, CSS, and JavaScript via Django&#x27;s Jinja2 template backend, providing fast initial loads and search-engine-friendly pages without a separate single-page application build pipeline.</p>
<p>The application tier comprises a Django 5.2.15 project partitioned into domain applications: users (identity, profiles, messaging, notifications), bookings (scheduling and status workflow), reviews (ratings), and escrow (wallet and payment holds). Gunicorn serves the WSGI application; Nginx terminates HTTP on port 80, serves static and media files, and reverse-proxies dynamic requests to Gunicorn.</p>
<p>The data tier combines PostgreSQL 15 for durable relational storage with Redis 7 for session storage and query caching. Django middleware enforces security headers, session management, CSRF validation, and authentication on every request before view logic executes—a fail-closed posture consistent with OWASP guidance.</p>


    <div class="figure-box">
      <table><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Technology</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr><tr><td>Presentation</td><td>Jinja2 templates + Nginx</td><td>UI rendering, static/media offload</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>Django 5.2 + Gunicorn</td><td>Business logic, auth, admin</td></tr><tr><td>Persistence</td><td>PostgreSQL 15</td><td>Users, bookings, escrow ledger</td></tr><tr><td>Cache / sessions</td><td>Redis 7 + django-redis</td><td>Worker search cache, sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Orchestration</td><td>Docker Compose 3.8</td><td>Reproducible multi-service deployment</td></tr></table>
    </div>
<div class="figure-caption">Table 1: Three-tier architecture and deployment components</div>

<h2>4. Business Model</h2>
<p>Sustainable marketplace design requires revenue without undermining either side of the platform. ProTech implements a hybrid model: transaction commissions deducted at escrow release, and tiered monthly subscriptions for workers seeking greater visibility.</p>
<p>The commission engine is live in code. When a worker completes a booking, release_escrow() calculates a platform fee from ESCROW_PLATFORM_FEE_PERCENT (default five percent), credits the worker&#x27;s wallet net of that fee, and records WalletTransaction audit rows. Production-oriented tiered rates are specified for micro-jobs (eight percent or minimum three currency units), standard jobs (five percent), and commercial contracts above five hundred units (three and a half percent).</p>
<p>Worker subscriptions—Starter (free), Professional ($29/month), and Premium ($59/month)—are documented with search-ranking benefits and commission discounts but remain a planned increment rather than implemented billing. Illustrative twenty-four-month growth projections model traffic, registered accounts, completed jobs, and monthly recurring revenue from combined commission and subscription streams.</p>


    <div class="figure-box">
      <table><tr><th>Plan</th><th>Price</th><th>Visibility</th><th>Commission discount</th></tr><tr><td>Starter</td><td>Free</td><td>Basic listing; capped bookings</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Professional</td><td>$29/mo</td><td>Priority above Starter</td><td>−1% (4% effective)</td></tr><tr><td>Premium</td><td>$59/mo</td><td>Featured placement</td><td>−2% (3% effective)</td></tr></table>
    </div>
<div class="figure-caption">Table 2: Worker subscription tiers (specified; billing not yet implemented)</div>

</div>
<div class="page-number">1</div>

<div class="journal-header">
