  <div>Manye Patrice | June 2026</div>
</div>

<div class="two-col-layout">

<h2>5. Authentication and Security</h2>
<p>Authentication combines Django&#x27;s session framework with a custom EmailBackend allowing login forms to accept email addresses as identifiers. Passwords are hashed with PBKDF2 through Django&#x27;s default validators. Registration creates either a client User or a User paired with a Worker profile depending on form submission type. Password reset uses Django&#x27;s tokenised email flow; development environments print reset URLs to the console.</p>
<p>Authorisation is enforced through a user_type field on the extended User model and a @user_type_required decorator that redirects unauthorised role access before protected views render. Clients access browse_workers, favorites, and booking flows; workers access job acceptance and status updates; superusers access Django Admin for content management.</p>


    <div class="figure-box">
      <table><tr><th>Threat (OWASP)</th><th>Control</th><th>Implementation</th></tr><tr><td>Broken access control</td><td>RBAC decorator</td><td>users/decorators.py</td></tr><tr><td>Injection</td><td>ORM parameterisation</td><td>Django ORM throughout</td></tr><tr><td>Cryptographic failures</td><td>PBKDF2 hashing</td><td>Django auth defaults</td></tr><tr><td>CSRF</td><td>CsrfViewMiddleware</td><td>csrf_field() in templates</td></tr><tr><td>XSS</td><td>Template auto-escaping</td><td>Jinja2/Django templates</td></tr><tr><td>Security misconfiguration</td><td>Environment secrets</td><td>.env excluded from git</td></tr></table>
    </div>
<div class="figure-caption">Table 3: Security controls mapped to OWASP categories</div>

<h3>5.1 Contrast with Prior PHP Prototype</h3>
<p>The earlier PHP/MySQL prototype implemented bcrypt hashing, MySQLi prepared statements, and PHP session roles within an XAMPP environment. While pedagogically valuable, it lacked integrated admin tooling, migration management, escrow logic, containerised deployment, automated testing, and caching. The Django rewrite consolidates these capabilities and eliminates the academic demo-mode bypass in favour of seed_demo management command and Docker entrypoint seeding—preserving demonstration convenience without alternate authentication code paths.</p>

<h2>6. Escrow Payment Subsystem</h2>
<p>Trust in online service exchange depends partly on payment timing. ProTech holds client funds in escrow until work is verified complete, reducing upfront risk for clients and non-payment risk for workers. The subsystem resides in escrow/services.py, keeping views thin and financial rules unit-testable.</p>
<p>The workflow proceeds as follows: (1) the client deposits demonstration funds into a UserWallet; (2) the client creates a Booking in pending status; (3) fund_escrow() debits the wallet and creates an EscrowHold linked one-to-one to the booking; (4) upon worker completion, release_escrow() computes platform_fee, credits worker_payout, updates EscrowVault aggregates, and writes WalletTransaction rows; (5) cancellation triggers refund_escrow() returning held amounts to the client wallet.</p>

<div class="code-box">users ──1:1── UserWallet
users ──1:1── Worker (professionals)
users ──1:N── Booking ──1:1── EscrowHold
Booking ──1:1── Review (post-completion)
EscrowHold ──N:1── WalletTransaction (audit trail)
EscrowVault (singleton) — platform-wide held/released totals</div>
<div class="figure-caption">Fig. 1: Core entity relationships (simplified ER view)</div>

<p>All monetary mutations execute inside database transactions with Decimal quantisation, preventing floating-point rounding errors. The service interface is structured so a production deployment could swap the internal ledger for Stripe Payment Intents and Connect webhooks without rewriting view layers.</p>

<h2>7. Data Model and Booking Workflow</h2>
<p>The relational schema centres on User and Worker profiles, ServiceCategory taxonomy, and Booking records linking clients to workers. Review attaches one-to-one to completed bookings. Supplementary Message, Notification, Favorite, and WorkerAvailability models support engagement beyond minimal booking forms.</p>
<p>Bookings progress through a finite state machine: pending → confirmed → in_progress → completed or cancelled. Escrow funding may occur while a booking is pending; release or refund hooks align with payment_status transitions. Integration tests verify that funding escrow debits the client wallet and sets payment_status to escrow_held.</p>


    <div class="figure-box">
      <table><tr><th>Booking status</th><th>Actor</th><th>Escrow interaction</th></tr><tr><td>pending</td><td>Worker accepts/rejects</td><td>Client may fund escrow</td></tr><tr><td>confirmed / in_progress</td><td>Worker updates</td><td>Funds held if funded</td></tr><tr><td>completed</td><td>Worker marks done</td><td>release_escrow(); fee deducted</td></tr><tr><td>cancelled</td><td>Either party</td><td>refund_escrow() if held</td></tr></table>
    </div>
<div class="figure-caption">Table 4: Booking states and escrow coupling</div>

</div>
